// Turkish counterpart of skill-suggest-eval.jsonl — the SAME 53 cases in the
// same order with the same labels, written the way they would actually be
// typed here.
//
// Why a parallel file rather than Turkish cases mixed into the English one:
// mixing them makes the language effect unmeasurable. A single blended set
// gives one number that moved for two reasons at once — different phrasing AND
// a different case mix — and nothing can separate them afterwards. Kept 1:1,
// the DELTA between the two runs is the language effect, and nothing else.
//
//   wstack skill-suggest --eval .wrongstack/skill-suggest-eval.jsonl --sweep
//   wstack skill-suggest --eval .wrongstack/skill-suggest-eval-tr.jsonl --sweep
//
// Why this matters here: TypeSafe documents English as the accuracy optimum and
// other languages as "work, with reduced reliability". Every gate question and
// every Choice rubric this feature sends is in English — the roster's triggers
// and descriptions are English too — while the request in `state` will usually
// be Turkish. Thresholds calibrated only on the English set are calibrated on
// the case that does not happen.
//
// If the Turkish run's `missed` rate is materially higher at the same
// thresholds, the honest fixes are, in order: lower `gateThreshold` for this
// deployment; or translate the three gate questions; or accept the loss and
// write it down. Do NOT split the difference by averaging the two runs — that
// produces a threshold that is wrong for both.
//
// DRAFT, same as the English file: every `gold` is inherited from it, which
// inherited them from skill frontmatter rather than from how you actually work.
// Fix a label in BOTH files or the pair stops being comparable.

